#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:9
msgid "Using ``turtlesim``, ``ros2``, and ``rqt``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:11
msgid ""
"**Goal:** Install and use the turtlesim package and rqt tools to prepare for "
"upcoming tutorials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:13
msgid "**Tutorial level:** Beginner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:15
msgid "**Time:** 15 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:19
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:22
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:24
msgid ""
"Turtlesim is a lightweight simulator for learning ROS 2. It illustrates what "
"ROS 2 does at the most basic level to give you an idea of what you will do "
"with a real robot or a robot simulation later on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:27
msgid ""
"The ros2 tool is how the user manages, introspects, and interacts with a ROS "
"system. It supports multiple commands that target different aspects of the "
"system and its operation. One might use it to start a node, set a parameter, "
"listen to a topic, and many more. The ros2 tool is part of the core ROS 2 "
"installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:32
msgid ""
"rqt is a graphical user interface (GUI) tool for ROS 2. Everything done in "
"rqt can be done on the command line, but rqt provides a more user-friendly "
"way to manipulate ROS 2 elements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:35
msgid ""
"This tutorial touches upon core ROS 2 concepts, like nodes, topics, and "
"services. All of these concepts will be elaborated on in later tutorials; "
"for now, you will simply set up the tools and get a feel for them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:39
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:41
msgid ""
"The previous tutorial, :doc:`../Configuring-ROS2-Environment`, will show you "
"how to set up your environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:44
msgid "Tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:47
msgid "1 Install turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:49
msgid ""
"As always, start by sourcing your setup files in a new terminal, as "
"described in the :doc:`previous tutorial <../Configuring-ROS2-Environment>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:51
msgid "Install the turtlesim package for your ROS 2 distro:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:55
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:146
msgid "Ubuntu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:57
msgid ""
"$ sudo apt update\n"
"$ sudo apt install ros-rolling-turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:62
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:153
msgid "RHEL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:64
msgid "$ sudo dnf install ros-rolling-turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:68
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:159
msgid "macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:70
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:74
msgid ""
"As long as the archive you installed ROS 2 from contains the "
"``ros_tutorials`` repository, you should already have turtlesim installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:72
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:163
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:76
msgid ""
"To check if the package is installed, run the following command, which "
"should return a list of turtlesim's executables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:78
msgid ""
"$ ros2 pkg executables turtlesim\n"
"turtlesim draw_square\n"
"turtlesim mimic\n"
"turtlesim turtle_teleop_key\n"
"turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:87
msgid "2 Start turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:89
msgid "To start turtlesim, enter the following command in your terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:91
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node\n"
"[INFO] [turtlesim]: Starting turtlesim with node name /turtlesim\n"
"[INFO] [turtlesim]: Spawning turtle [turtle1] at x=[5.544445], y=[5.544445], "
"theta=[0.000000]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:97
msgid ""
"Under the command, you will see messages from the node. There you can see "
"the default turtle's name and the coordinates where it spawns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:100
msgid "The simulator window should appear, with a random turtle in the center."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:106
msgid "3 Use turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:108
msgid "Open a new terminal and source ROS 2 again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:110
msgid "Now you will run a new node to control the turtle in the first node:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:112
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtle_teleop_key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:116
msgid ""
"At this point you should have three windows open: a terminal running "
"``turtlesim_node``, a terminal running ``turtle_teleop_key`` and the "
"turtlesim window. Arrange these windows so that you can see the turtlesim "
"window, but also have the terminal running ``turtle_teleop_key`` active so "
"that you can control the turtle in turtlesim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:119
msgid ""
"Use the arrow keys on your keyboard to control the turtle. It will move "
"around the screen, using its attached \"pen\" to draw the path it followed "
"so far."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:124
msgid ""
"Pressing an arrow key will only cause the turtle to move a short distance "
"and then stop. This is because, realistically, you wouldn't want a robot to "
"continue carrying on an instruction if, for example, the operator lost the "
"connection to the robot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:127
msgid ""
"You can see the nodes, and their associated topics, services, and actions, "
"using the ``list`` subcommands of the respective commands:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:129
msgid ""
"$ ros2 node list\n"
"$ ros2 topic list\n"
"$ ros2 service list\n"
"$ ros2 action list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:136
msgid ""
"You will learn more about these concepts in the coming tutorials. Since the "
"goal of this tutorial is only to get a general overview of turtlesim, you "
"will use rqt to call some of the turtlesim services and interact with "
"``turtlesim_node``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:140
msgid "4 Install rqt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:142
msgid "Open a new terminal to install ``rqt`` and its plugins:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:148
msgid ""
"$ sudo apt update\n"
"$ sudo apt install ros-rolling-rqt ros-rolling-rqt-common-plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:155
msgid "$ sudo dnf install 'ros-rolling-rqt*'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:161
msgid ""
"The standard archive for installing ROS 2 on macOS contains ``rqt`` and its "
"plugins, so you should already have ``rqt`` installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:165
msgid ""
"The standard archive for installing ROS 2 on Windows contains ``rqt`` and "
"its plugins, so you should already have ``rqt`` installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:167
msgid "To run rqt:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:169
msgid "$ rqt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:174
msgid "5 Use rqt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:176
msgid ""
"When running rqt for the first time, the window will be blank. No worries; "
"just select **Plugins** > **Services** > **Service Caller** from the menu "
"bar at the top."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:181
msgid ""
"It may take some time for rqt to locate all the plugins. If you click on "
"**Plugins** but don't see **Services** or any other options, you should "
"close rqt and enter the command ``rqt --force-discover`` in your terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:186
msgid ""
"Use the refresh button to the left of the **Service** dropdown list to "
"ensure all the services of your turtlesim node are available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:188
msgid ""
"Click on the **Service** dropdown list to see turtlesim's services, and "
"select the ``/spawn`` service."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:191
msgid "5.1 Try the spawn service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:193
msgid ""
"Let's use rqt to call the ``/spawn`` service. You can guess from its name "
"that ``/spawn`` will create another turtle in the turtlesim window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:196
msgid ""
"Give the new turtle a unique name, like ``turtle2``, by double-clicking "
"between the empty single quotes in the **Expression** column. You can see "
"that this expression corresponds to the value of **name** and is of type "
"**string**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:199
msgid ""
"Next enter some valid coordinates at which to spawn the new turtle, like ``x "
"= 1.0`` and ``y = 1.0``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:205
msgid ""
"If you try to spawn a new turtle with the same name as an existing turtle, "
"like the default ``turtle1``, you will get an error message in the terminal "
"running ``turtlesim_node``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:207
msgid "[ERROR] [turtlesim]: A turtle named [turtle1] already exists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:211
msgid ""
"To spawn ``turtle2``, you then need to call the service by clicking the "
"**Call** button on the upper right side of the rqt window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:213
msgid ""
"If the service call was successful, you should see a new turtle (again with "
"a random design) spawn at the coordinates you input for **x** and **y**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:215
msgid ""
"If you refresh the service list in rqt, you will also see that now there are "
"services related to the new turtle, ``/turtle2/...``, in addition to ``/"
"turtle1/...``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:218
msgid "5.2 Try the set_pen service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:220
msgid "Now let's give ``turtle1`` a unique pen using the ``/set_pen`` service:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:224
msgid ""
"The values for **r**, **g** and **b**, which are between 0 and 255, set the "
"color of the pen ``turtle1`` draws with, and **width** sets the thickness of "
"the line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:226
msgid ""
"To have ``turtle1`` draw with a distinct red line, change the value of **r** "
"to 255, and the value of **width** to 5. Don't forget to call the service "
"after updating the values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:229
msgid ""
"If you return to the terminal where ``turtle_teleop_key`` is running and "
"press the arrow keys, you will see ``turtle1``'s pen has changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:233
msgid ""
"You've probably also noticed that there's no way to move ``turtle2``. That's "
"because there is no teleop node for ``turtle2``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:237
msgid "6 Remapping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:239
msgid ""
"You need a second teleop node in order to control ``turtle2``. However, if "
"you try to run the same command as before, you will notice that this one "
"also controls ``turtle1``. The way to change this behavior is by remapping "
"the ``cmd_vel`` topic and the ``rotate_absolute`` action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:243
msgid "In a new terminal, source ROS 2, and run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:245
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtle_teleop_key --ros-args --remap turtle1/cmd_vel:"
"=turtle2/cmd_vel --remap turtle1/rotate_absolute:=turtle2/rotate_absolute"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:250
msgid ""
"Now, you can move ``turtle2`` when this terminal is active, and ``turtle1`` "
"when the other terminal running ``turtle_teleop_key`` is active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:255
msgid "7 Close turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:257
msgid ""
"To stop the simulation, you can enter ``Ctrl + C`` in the ``turtlesim_node`` "
"terminal, and ``q`` in the ``turtle_teleop_key`` terminals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:260
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:262
msgid ""
"Using turtlesim and rqt is a great way to learn the core concepts of ROS 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:265
msgid "Next steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:267
msgid ""
"Now that you have turtlesim and rqt up and running, and an idea of how they "
"work, let's dive into the first core ROS 2 concept with the next tutorial, :"
"doc:`../../ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/"
"Understanding-ROS2-Nodes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:270
msgid "Related content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim.rst:272
msgid ""
"The turtlesim package can be found in the `ros_tutorials <https://github.com/"
"ros/ros_tutorials/tree/rolling/turtlesim>`_ repo."
msgstr ""
